From 49aa685fabfce62cce8e1b06d40eda766fbe9b28 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domingo Alvarez Duarte <mingo@Domingos-MacBook-Air.local>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 14:30:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] feat(generate): report rules that can never appear in a
 tree
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two silent failure modes, both of which produce a parser that accepts less than
the grammar says and says nothing about it:

- A rule that nothing reaches from the start rule was dropped by
  `InputGrammar::normalize` without a word. Dropping it is right (it is how a
  grammar with leftover rules still generates), but the author is the one
  person who needs to know.

- A rule whose every production needs a rule that can never match derives no
  sentence, so no state can ever reduce to it: the classic missing base case
  (`a: a b;` with no `a: b;`) or a rule that needs a token nothing lexes. This
  one generates a parser happily, and the rule simply never appears in a tree.
  Bison calls the same input "nonterminal useless in grammar".

The first is reported where the drop happens. The second is a worklist over the
flattened productions, linear in the number of steps: a rule becomes productive
when one of its productions has no unproductive steps left, where a token or an
external token always counts as productive. Both are `Diagnostic`s, so they
print as warnings, reach `--json-summary`, and cost nothing when the grammar is
healthy (postgres16: 4.01 s / 80 MB, unchanged).

Measured on the 92-grammar corpus already used for the other commits: 10
grammars gain one or more `not reachable` warnings, and every one of them is a
dead rule (bash's `string_expansion`, cpp's `_old_style_function_definition`,
the whole JSX subtree of the TypeScript grammar, …). `cannot match anything` is
rarer: the only corpus grammar it fires on is `conflicting_precedence`, which
genuinely has no base case, plus the two shapes added below as tests. It does
not fire on any of the 358 bison/flex grammars translated by `g2tsg`, nor on
postgres16.
